<BaseLayout
  title="About — China Layover Guide"
  description="Why China Layover Guide exists and how we keep the information accurate."
  canonicalPath="/about/"
>
  <article class="prose prose-slate mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p>
      China Layover Guide exists for one traveler: the person with 10, 24, or 72 hours in China and
      no idea whether they can leave the airport. Most can — thanks to the 240-hour visa-free
      transit policy — and a short layover can become one of the best parts of a trip.
    </p>
    <p>
      We focus on the three things that make or break a China stopover: the immigration rules,
      staying connected (eSIM before you land), and paying for things. Then we point you at
      realistic city routes so you actually see something.
    </p>
    <h2>Accuracy</h2>
    <p>
      Immigration and payment rules change. Every factual page carries a &ldquo;last updated&rdquo;
      date and links to official sources. Always verify with the National Immigration
      Administration, your airline, and your bank before you travel.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
